var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Causal-loop-diagrams,-and-what-a-Bayesian-network-does-to-one","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nFour polarities, and why not three\nA sketch, and its loops\nReading a sign off a table\nWhy this is a labelling and not a convention\nTwo readings of one network\nWhere the loops come back\nThe two kinds of loop\nHolding a sketch against a model\nCoarsening, and the arithmetic it needs\nWhat a polarity cannot carry\nReferences","title":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Overview","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"A causal loop diagram is the first thing drawn when a group of people describe a system: factors joined by arrows, a sign on each arrow, loops closing back on themselves. Baez formalises one as a graph whose edges are labelled by elements of a monoid of polarities, which makes a labelling the same thing as a functor from the free category on the graph into the delooping of that monoid (Baez 2024); with Chaudhuri he goes on to three kinds of morphism between labelled graphs – refining, simplifying, finding motifs – and to a homology that counts the feedback a composite acquires that neither part had (Baez and Chaudhuri 2025).\n\nA Bayesian network carries much more than a sign per arrow, and one thing less: it must be acyclic, so it cannot hold a loop at all. This vignette walks the translation in both directions and is as interested in what it loses as in what it keeps.\n\nusing CausalLoopDiagrams\nusing BayesianNetworks\n\n# The figures go in `<vignette>_figures/`, which `scripts/sync_vignettes.jl` carries into\n# `docs/src/tutorials/` alongside the Markdown, so the same relative paths work in the\n# vignette tree and in the manual. Not `<vignette>_files/`: quarto owns that name and\n# expects only its own subdirectories in it.\nconst FIGURES = \"01_causal_loop_diagrams_figures\"\nmkpath(FIGURES)\n\nfunction draw(x, file; dpi = 130, kw...)\n    graph = to_graphviz(x; graph_attrs = Dict(:dpi => string(dpi)), kw...)\n    open(joinpath(FIGURES, file), \"w\") do io\n        Graphviz.run_graphviz(io, graph; format = \"png\")\n    end\n    return nothing\nend\nnothing # hide","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Four-polarities,-and-why-not-three","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"The basic case is the sign group, {+, -}. Adding an element for “indeterminate” gives Baez’s three-element monoid {+, 0, -}. This package uses four, because a conditional probability table distinguishes two things that monoid writes alike:\n\npolarities()\n\n(0, +, -, ?)\n\nNO_EFFECT is the link along which nothing moves – a table that does not depend on that parent at all – and INDETERMINATE is a link whose direction the diagram does not determine. On a whiteboard the two are indistinguishable; in a table they are different facts, and the first is a finding rather than an absence of one.\n\nThe four values are the subsets {0}, {+}, {-} and {0, +, -} of the sign hyperfield, and those four subsets are closed under its multiplication and its (multivalued) addition. So they form a commutative rig: a sub-rig of the power-set rig Baez builds from the sign hyperfield. Multiplication composes links along a path and addition combines links in parallel.\n\nprintln(\"composition along a path:\")\nfor a in polarities()\n    println(\"  \", [a * b for b in polarities()])\nend\nprintln(\"links in parallel:\")\nfor a in polarities()\n    println(\"  \", [a + b for b in polarities()])\nend\nprintln(\"units: one = \", one(Polarity), \", zero = \", zero(Polarity))\n\ncomposition along a path:\n  Polarity[0, 0, 0, 0]\n  Polarity[0, +, -, ?]\n  Polarity[0, -, +, ?]\n  Polarity[0, ?, ?, ?]\nlinks in parallel:\n  Polarity[0, +, -, ?]\n  Polarity[+, +, ?, ?]\n  Polarity[-, ?, -, ?]\n  Polarity[?, ?, ?, ?]\nunits: one = +, zero = 0\n\nTwo things to read off those tables. NO_EFFECT is absorbing for composition – a path through a dead link is dead – and the unit for addition. And a positive link beside a negative one adds to ?: two pathways that disagree, with nothing in the formalism to say which wins. That is not a gap in the implementation, it is what a sign is.","title":"Four polarities, and why not three"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#A-sketch,-and-its-loops","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Here is a seasonal tree disease as a practitioner would sketch it. Inoculum meets host tissue and infection follows; infection replenishes the inoculum for next season and damages the host; and a damaged host carries less tissue to infect. delayed marks the links that do not act within the period.\n\nsketch = causal_loop_diagram([(:Inoculum, :Infection) => POSITIVE,\n                              (:Infection, :Damage) => POSITIVE,\n                              (:Damage, :Tissue) => NEGATIVE,\n                              delayed((:Tissue, :Infection) => POSITIVE),\n                              delayed((:Infection, :Inoculum) => POSITIVE)])\ndraw(sketch, \"sketch.png\")\nsketch\n\n<div class=\"c-set\">\n\nCausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagram {Factor:4, Link:5, Label:0, Sign:0, Flag:0}\n\nFactor factor_name\n1 Inoculum\n2 Infection\n3 Damage\n4 Tissue\n\nLink link_source link_target link_polarity link_delay\n1 1 2 + false\n2 2 3 + false\n3 3 4 - false\n4 4 2 + true\n5 2 1 + true\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"fig-sketch\">\n\n<img src=\"01causalloopdiagramsfigures/sketch.png\" style=\"width:70.0%\" />\n\nFigure 1: A seasonal disease as a causal loop diagram. Green is positive, red negative; a dashed link crosses a delay.\n\n</div>\n\nforeach(println, loops(sketch))\nloop_summary(sketch)\n\nInoculum -> Infection -> Inoculum (+, reinforcing, delayed)\nInfection -> Damage -> Tissue -> Infection (-, balancing, delayed)\n\n(loops = 2, reinforcing = 1, balancing = 1, undetermined = 0, inactive = 0, delayed = 2, instantaneous = 0)\n\nTwo loops that pull against each other. The inoculum loop is reinforcing: a heavy season leaves a heavy inoculum. The damage loop is balancing: an epidemic that defoliates its host removes what it needs. A loop’s polarity is the product of the signs around it, and because the rig is commutative it does not depend on where the loop is cut –\n\n(loop_polarity(sketch, [:Infection, :Damage, :Tissue]),\n loop_polarity(sketch, [:Tissue, :Infection, :Damage]))\n\n(-, -)\n\n– which is what makes it a property of the loop rather than of how it was written down.","title":"A sketch, and its loops"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Reading-a-sign-off-a-table","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Nothing so far has needed probabilities. The bridge to the networks is polarity, which reads a sign off a conditional probability table by stochastic monotonicity in the declared order of the states: the child’s distribution rises with the parent if its upper tail never shrinks, for every configuration of the other parents.\n\nrain = FiniteAxis(:Rain, [:dry, :wet])\ngrass = FiniteAxis(:Grass, [:brown, :green])\n\n(polarity(cpt(rain, grass, [0.9 0.1; 0.2 0.8]), :Rain),\n polarity(cpt(rain, grass, [0.1 0.9; 0.8 0.2]), :Rain),\n polarity(cpt(rain, grass, [0.5 0.5; 0.5 0.5]), :Rain))\n\n(+, -, 0)\n\nDominance is a stronger requirement than a rising mean, and deliberately so. The table below has damage increasing in load on average, but the middle row does not dominate the first – less chance of none and less chance of severe – so there is no one direction to report:\n\nload = FiniteAxis(:Load, [:low, :medium, :high])\ndamage = FiniteAxis(:Damage, [:none, :some, :severe])\nspread = cpt(load, damage, [0.6 0.1 0.3; 0.2 0.7 0.1; 0.1 0.2 0.7])\npolarity(spread, :Load)\n\n?\n\nAn interaction between two parents comes out the same way, which is the honest answer: the sign of one link depends on the state of another factor, and a label on an edge has nowhere to put that.\n\nallele = FiniteAxis(:Allele, [:absent, :present])\nexposure = FiniteAxis(:Exposure, [:low, :high])\nharm = FiniteAxis(:Harm, [:no, :yes])\ntable = Array{Float64}(undef, 2, 2, 2)\ntable[1, 1, :] = [0.8, 0.2]      # no allele, low exposure\ntable[1, 2, :] = [0.4, 0.6]      # no allele, high exposure: worse\ntable[2, 1, :] = [0.9, 0.1]      # allele, low exposure\ntable[2, 2, :] = [0.95, 0.05]    # allele, high exposure: better\ninteracting = cpt([allele, exposure], harm, table)\n\n(polarity(interacting, :Exposure), polarity(interacting, :Allele))\n\n(?, -)","title":"Reading a sign off a table"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Why-this-is-a-labelling-and-not-a-convention","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Monotone kernels compose. If the child’s distribution rises with the parent and a further kernel is monotone in the child, then the composite rises with the parent – so the polarity of a path really is the product of the polarities of its links, which is exactly what Baez’s lemma requires of a labelling. It is worth checking rather than asserting:\n\nsheep = FiniteAxis(:Sheep, [:few, :many])\nfirst_links = [cpt(rain, grass, [0.9 0.1; 0.2 0.8]), cpt(rain, grass, [0.1 0.9; 0.8 0.2])]\nsecond_links = [cpt(grass, sheep, [0.7 0.3; 0.25 0.75]),\n                cpt(grass, sheep, [0.3 0.7; 0.75 0.25])]\n\nfor f in first_links, g in second_links\n    composite = polarity(compose_kernel(f, g), :Rain)\n    product = polarity(f, :Rain) * polarity(g, :Grass)\n    println(polarity(f, :Rain), \" then \", polarity(g, :Grass), \" gives \", composite,\n            \"; the product is \", product, \"  (\",\n            composite === product ? \"agree\" : \"DISAGREE\", \")\")\nend\n\n+ then + gives +; the product is +  (agree)\n+ then - gives -; the product is -  (agree)\n- then + gives -; the product is -  (agree)\n- then - gives +; the product is +  (agree)","title":"Why this is a labelling and not a convention"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Two-readings-of-one-network","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"A causal loop diagram’s arrow claims causation, but diagrams get drawn in workshops from things that were seen to move together. Those are different questions, and causal_loop_diagram will answer either one about a model whose tables are bound.\n\nThe reference habitat network is a tree with no confounding in it, and the two readings agree on every link:\n\nhabitat = reference_habitat_model()\nstructural = causal_loop_diagram(habitat)\nobservational = causal_loop_diagram(habitat; mode = :observational)\ndraw(structural, \"habitat.png\")\ncompare_diagrams(structural, observational)\n\nDiagramComparison: 6 agree, 0 disagree, 0 only in the first, 0 only in the second\n\n<div id=\"fig-habitat\">\n\n<img src=\"01causalloopdiagramsfigures/habitat.png\" style=\"width:85.0%\" />\n\nFigure 2: The reference habitat network read as a causal loop diagram. It is acyclic, as every Bayesian network must be, so it has no feedback in it.\n\n</div>\n\nNow a model with confounding. Fertiliser goes on poor soil; soil drives yield harder than fertiliser does. Both mechanisms are monotone, so neither link is indeterminate:\n\nbn = bayesnet(:Soil => [:poor, :rich], :Fertiliser => [:none, :some],\n              :Yield => [:low, :high];\n              mechanisms = [:Fertiliser => :Soil, :Yield => (:Soil, :Fertiliser)])\nyield = Array{Float64}(undef, 2, 2, 2)\nyield[1, 1, :] = [0.9, 0.1]      # poor soil, no fertiliser\nyield[1, 2, :] = [0.7, 0.3]      # poor soil, fertiliser\nyield[2, 1, :] = [0.3, 0.7]      # rich soil, no fertiliser\nyield[2, 2, :] = [0.1, 0.9]      # rich soil, fertiliser\nfarm = bind_cpt(BayesModel(bn),\n                [:Soil => [0.5, 0.5], :Fertiliser => [0.1 0.9; 0.9 0.1],\n                 :Yield => yield])\n\ncompare_diagrams(causal_loop_diagram(farm),\n                 causal_loop_diagram(farm; mode = :observational))\n\nDiagramComparison: 2 agree, 1 disagree, 0 only in the first, 0 only in the second\n  Fertiliser -> Yield: + against -\n\nApplying fertiliser raises the yield and observing it lowers the expectation, because fertiliser marks poor soil. A diagram drawn from co-movement has that link signed backwards while claiming to be causal, and the disagreement list is the check – computed, not argued.","title":"Two readings of one network"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Where-the-loops-come-back","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"The sketch had two loops; the networks above have none, because that is what acyclicity means. The loops are not lost, though: in a dynamic network they are carried by the lags. A transition template is an ordinary acyclic network over variables named X and X[t-1], and quotienting the time index puts the feedback back.\n\ngrazing = vegetation_herbivore_model()\ntemplate = causal_loop_diagram(transition_network(grazing))\ndraw(template, \"template.png\")\n(nfactors = nfactors(template), loops = length(loops(template)))\n\n(nfactors = 4, loops = 0)\n\n<div id=\"fig-template\">\n\n<img src=\"01causalloopdiagramsfigures/template.png\" style=\"width:85.0%\" />\n\nFigure 3: The transition template as a causal loop diagram, with the lagged variables still separate factors. Acyclic, so no loops.\n\n</div>\n\ncollapsed = causal_loop_diagram(grazing)\ndraw(collapsed, \"collapsed.png\")\nforeach(println, loops(collapsed))\nloop_summary(collapsed)\n\nVegetation -> Vegetation (+, reinforcing, delayed)\nVegetation -> Herbivores -> Vegetation (-, balancing, delayed)\n\n(loops = 2, reinforcing = 1, balancing = 1, undetermined = 0, inactive = 0, delayed = 2, instantaneous = 0)\n\n<div id=\"fig-collapsed\">\n\n<img src=\"01causalloopdiagramsfigures/collapsed.png\" style=\"width:55.0%\" />\n\nFigure 4: The same template with the time index collapsed. The loops are back, and the links that crossed a slice boundary are the dashed ones.\n\n</div>\n\nGrazing comes out balancing – more vegetation, more herbivores, less vegetation next season – and the sign was computed from the conditional probability tables rather than asserted. Vegetation persisting from one slice to the next comes back as a self-link, which is the shortest feedback loop there is.","title":"Where the loops come back"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#The-two-kinds-of-loop","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"That distinction is the one that decides what can be modelled, and it is why a link carries a delay flag at all.\n\nmixed = causal_loop_diagram([(:Price, :Supply) => POSITIVE,\n                             (:Supply, :Price) => NEGATIVE,\n                             (:Stock, :Harvest) => POSITIVE,\n                             delayed((:Harvest, :Stock) => NEGATIVE)])\nprintln(\"delayed:       \", delayed_loops(mixed))\nprintln(\"instantaneous: \", instantaneous_loops(mixed))\n\ndelayed:       Loop[Stock -> Harvest -> Stock (-, balancing, delayed)]\ninstantaneous: Loop[Price -> Supply -> Price (-, balancing, instantaneous)]\n\nA delayed loop is one a dynamic Bayesian network holds as it stands. An instantaneous loop asserts that everything in it happens at once; no Bayesian network holds one, and unrolling does not help, because there is no slice boundary for the closing link to cross. Representing one means changing the model – splitting the factor into stages, or admitting a delay the sketch left out.","title":"The two kinds of loop"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Holding-a-sketch-against-a-model","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"The last use is the one that keeps a modelling exercise honest. Put the sketch beside the diagram read off the finished network, and the differences are a list of what the modelling did.\n\none_season = causal_loop_diagram([(:Inoculum, :Infection) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Infection, :Damage) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Damage, :Tissue) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Weather, :Infection) => POSITIVE])\ncompare_diagrams(sketch, one_season)\n\nDiagramComparison: 2 agree, 1 disagree, 2 only in the first, 1 only in the second\n  Damage -> Tissue: - against +\n  Tissue -(+)-> Infection: only in the first\n  Infection -(+)-> Inoculum: only in the first\n  Weather -(+)-> Infection: only in the second\n\nThree kinds of entry, and each is worth a question. A link only in the sketch is feedback the network cut. A link only in the network is something the sketch had not thought of. A link both have with different signs is a disagreement that one of the two has got wrong.","title":"Holding a sketch against a model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#Coarsening,-and-the-arithmetic-it-needs","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Baez’s simplifying morphism is the push-forward of edge labels along a map of graphs, and it is the operation that forces the labels to be addable: when two factors merge, the links between them and a third become parallel and have to be combined.\n\npathways = causal_loop_diagram([(:Shade, :Growth) => NEGATIVE,\n                                (:Shelter, :Growth) => POSITIVE])\ncanopy = coarsen(pathways, Dict(:Shade => :Canopy, :Shelter => :Canopy))\ncanopy\n\n<div class=\"c-set\">\n\nCausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagram {Factor:2, Link:1, Label:0, Sign:0, Flag:0}\n\nFactor factor_name\n1 Canopy\n2 Growth\n\nLink link_source link_target link_polarity link_delay\n1 1 2 ? false\n\n</div>\n\nTwo pathways of opposite sign become one link labelled ?. That is the right answer and a lossy one: the coarse diagram cannot say whether a canopy helps, and the fine one could not say which effect was bigger either. Merging two factors that were linked to each other turns that link into a self-link, which is a feedback loop of length one – not a defect of the operation, but what coarsening does to the detail inside a factor.","title":"Coarsening, and the arithmetic it needs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#What-a-polarity-cannot-carry","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"Worth stating plainly, because it is the reason a sketch is a sketch and a network is a model.\n\nNo magnitude. A diagram cannot say which of two loops through one factor wins. That is why Baez’s series moves on from monoids to rings, hyperfields and rigs with more in them than signs.\nNo interaction. A link whose direction depends on another factor comes out ?, and a lever worth more alongside an intervention than alone has no expression in a diagram at all.\nNo unordered variable. “Rises” needs an order on the states. A platform choice, a delivery route, a species identity has none, and causal_loop_diagram takes an unordered keyword so that the links touching one are reported as undetermined rather than guessed:\n\nguessing = causal_loop_diagram(habitat; unordered = [:Vegetation])\n(link_polarity(guessing, :SoilMoisture, :Vegetation),\n link_polarity(guessing, :Vegetation, :HabitatQuality),\n link_polarity(guessing, :Climate, :SoilMoisture))\n\n(?, ?, +)\n\nA diagram over a decision between incomparable options has very little to say, and saying so is the useful part.","title":"What a polarity cannot carry"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_causal_loop_diagrams/#References","page":"Causal loop diagrams, and what a Bayesian network does to one","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Baez2024\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBaez, John C. 2024. Polarities. Azimuth (blog), parts 1 to 6. https://johncarlosbaez.wordpress.com/2024/11/02/polarities/.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-BaezChaudhuri2025\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBaez, John C., and Adittya Chaudhuri. 2025. Graphs With Polarities. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.23375.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Polarities","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Polarities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Diagrams","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Diagrams"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reading-a-diagram","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Reading a diagram"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Loops","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Loops"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#From-a-Bayesian-network","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"From a Bayesian network"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Maps-between-diagrams","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Maps between diagrams"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Motifs","page":"API Reference","text":"Finding motifs is the third of Baez and Chaudhuri's morphisms between labelled graphs. A motif is itself a small labelled graph, and an occurrence of it is an embedding that respects the labels: the matching runs along the subset order on Polarity (issubset), so a motif link labelled INDETERMINATE matches a link of any sign.","title":"Motifs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-standard-shapes","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"The standard shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Canonical-form-and-the-census","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Canonical form and the census"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Drawing","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Drawing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Exceptions","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams","page":"API Reference","text":"CausalLoopDiagrams\n\nCausal loop diagrams as labelled graphs, and the bridge between them and the Bayesian networks of the ecorecipes ecosystem.\n\nA causal loop diagram is what a group of people draw on a whiteboard when they first describe a system: factors joined by arrows, each arrow marked with the direction in which it pushes, and loops closing back on themselves. Baez formalises one as a graph whose edges are labelled by elements of a monoid of polarities, which makes a labelling the same thing as a functor from the free category on the graph into the delooping of the monoid (Baez, 2024); Baez and Chaudhuri take it further, to three kinds of morphism between labelled graphs – refining, simplifying and finding motifs – and to a homology that counts the feedback loops a composite acquires that neither part had (Baez and Chaudhuri, 2025).\n\nA Bayesian network carries much more: a conditional probability table for every mechanism rather than a sign. It also carries less, in one specific way: it must be acyclic, so it cannot hold a loop at all.\n\nThis package is the two-way translation.\n\nPolarity is the label: POSITIVE, NEGATIVE, NO_EFFECT and INDETERMINATE, a commutative rig under composition along a path (*) and combination in parallel (+).\npolarity reads a polarity off a conditional probability table, by stochastic monotonicity in the declared order of the states. Monotone kernels compose, which is what makes the reading a labelling in Baez's sense rather than a convention: the polarity of a path really is the product of the polarities of its links.\ncausal_loop_diagram builds a diagram from a list of links – the sketch, drawn by hand, loops intact – or reads one off a BayesianNetworks.BayesModel, in either an interventional (:structural) or an observational reading.\nloops, delayed_loops and instantaneous_loops enumerate the feedback and classify it. The distinction the last two draw is the one that matters for modelling: a loop with a delay in it is a loop a dynamic Bayesian network holds as it stands, the delayed link becoming a lagged input, and causal_loop_diagram recovers it by collapsing the time index of a transition template. A loop with no delay asserts simultaneity and no Bayesian network holds it.\ncoarsen, collapse_lags and compare_diagrams are the maps between diagrams: the push-forward of labels along a map of factors, its time-index special case, and a link-by-link comparison of two labellings of one graph.\noccurrences and motif_census are the third of Baez and Chaudhuri's morphisms, finding motifs: a motif is itself a small labelled graph, an occurrence of it is a label-respecting embedding, and the census counts the shapes across a collection of diagrams. MOTIF_LIBRARY names the standard ones.\n\nWhat the translation discards is worth stating plainly, because it is the reason a diagram is a sketch and a network is a model. A polarity cannot hold a magnitude, so it cannot say which of two loops through the same factor wins. It cannot hold an interaction: a link whose sign depends on another parent comes out INDETERMINATE, and a quantity that is worth more alongside an intervention than alone has no expression in the diagram at all. And it cannot hold a variable whose states have no order, which is what most decisions between incomparable options are.\n\nPart of the ecorecipes compositional Bayesian-network ecosystem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.Polarity","page":"API Reference","text":"Polarity\n\nThe label on a link of a CausalLoopDiagram: one of POSITIVE, NEGATIVE, NO_EFFECT and INDETERMINATE.\n\nThe four values are the subsets {0}, {+}, {-} and {0, +, -} of the sign hyperfield, and they form a commutative rig under its operations (Baez, 2024): * composes links along a path (so one(Polarity) == POSITIVE) and + combines links in parallel (so zero(Polarity) == NO_EFFECT). Negation is multiplication by NEGATIVE.\n\nshow prints the symbol (polarity_symbol), so a vector of polarities reads as a row of signs.\n\njulia> POSITIVE * NEGATIVE\n-\n\njulia> NEGATIVE * NEGATIVE\n+\n\njulia> POSITIVE + NEGATIVE\n?\n\njulia> (one(Polarity), zero(Polarity))\n(+, 0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.Polarity"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.POSITIVE","page":"API Reference","text":"POSITIVE\n\nThe polarity of a link along which the child moves the same way as the parent: raising the parent raises the child in the sense of stochastic dominance (see polarity). It is the multiplicative unit of Polarity (one(Polarity)) and prints as +.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.POSITIVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.NEGATIVE","page":"API Reference","text":"NEGATIVE\n\nThe polarity of a link along which the child moves the opposite way to the parent. Prints as -.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.NEGATIVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.NO_EFFECT","page":"API Reference","text":"NO_EFFECT\n\nThe polarity of a link along which nothing moves: the table of the child does not depend on this parent at all. It is the additive unit of Polarity (zero(Polarity)) and absorbing for *, and prints as 0.\n\nBaez's three-element monoid {+, 0, -} writes 0 for an indeterminate sign and has no element for this one; here the two are INDETERMINATE and NO_EFFECT respectively, because a conditional probability table tells them apart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.NO_EFFECT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.INDETERMINATE","page":"API Reference","text":"INDETERMINATE\n\nThe polarity of a link with no consistent direction: the child depends on the parent, but whether it rises or falls depends on the other parents, or on which pair of parent levels is compared. Prints as ?.\n\nThis is the value a genuine interaction takes, and it is the honest label for a link out of a variable whose states carry no order at all (a treatment with several incomparable options, say); causal_loop_diagram takes an unordered keyword that marks such variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.INDETERMINATE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarities","page":"API Reference","text":"polarities() -> NTuple{4, Polarity}\n\nThe four polarities in the order NO_EFFECT, POSITIVE, NEGATIVE, INDETERMINATE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarities"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_symbol","page":"API Reference","text":"polarity_symbol(p::Polarity) -> String\n\nThe one-character form of a polarity, as the figures and tables print it: \"0\", \"+\", \"-\" or \"?\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_symbol"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_name","page":"API Reference","text":"polarity_name(p::Polarity) -> Symbol\n\nThe polarity as a word: :no_effect, :positive, :negative or :indeterminate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_name"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity-Tuple{FiniteKernel, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"polarity(k::FiniteKernel, position::Integer; atol = DEFAULT_ATOL) -> Polarity\npolarity(k::FiniteKernel, parent::Symbol; atol = DEFAULT_ATOL) -> Polarity\n\nThe polarity of the dependence of a kernel's child on one of its parents, named or given by its position in the domain: the direction in which the child's distribution moves as that parent rises through its states, with the other parents held fixed.\n\nThe test is stochastic monotonicity in the declared order of the states. Writing F(j | x) for the probability that the child is in state j or later given parent level x, the kernel is increasing in that parent when F(j | x) never decreases as x rises, for every j and every configuration of the other parents, and decreasing when it never increases. So\n\nPOSITIVE – increasing and not constant,\nNEGATIVE – decreasing and not constant,\nNO_EFFECT – the child's distribution does not depend on this parent,\nINDETERMINATE – neither, which is what an interaction with another parent looks like.\n\natol is the slack allowed in each comparison; it defaults to FiniteKernels' DEFAULT_ATOL.\n\nMonotone kernels compose, which is what makes this reading a labelling in the sense of (Baez, 2024): if the child's distribution rises with the parent and a further kernel is monotone in the child, the composite rises with the parent, so the polarity of a path is the product of the polarities of its links. That is a theorem, proved for one-parent kernels in exact arithmetic in proofs/CausalLoopDiagramsProofs/Theory/Composition.lean (reads_comp, and polarity_comp for the statement in the rig); the test suite checks the same identity numerically against compose_kernel on all four sign combinations, which is what stands behind the floating-point case and the atol slack below. The direction depends on the order the states were declared in; a variable whose states run from best to worst reads backwards, which is what the reversed keyword of causal_loop_diagram is for.\n\nBoth state orders matter, so a kernel over unordered states has no meaningful polarity; nothing here can detect that, and it is the caller's business to say so.\n\njulia> X = FiniteAxis(:Rain, [:dry, :wet]);\n\njulia> Y = FiniteAxis(:Grass, [:brown, :green]);\n\njulia> polarity(cpt(X, Y, [0.9 0.1; 0.2 0.8]), :Rain)\n+\n\njulia> polarity(cpt(X, Y, [0.5 0.5; 0.5 0.5]), :Rain)\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.SchCausalLoop","page":"API Reference","text":"SchCausalLoop\n\nSchema of a causal loop diagram: objects Factor and Link, homs link_source: Link -> Factor and link_target: Link -> Factor, attribute types Label, Sign and Flag, and attributes factor_name (on Factor), link_polarity and link_delay (on Link). There are no path equations.\n\nA self-link (link_source == link_target) is allowed and means what it says; so are parallel links between the same pair of factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.SchCausalLoop"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.AbstractCausalLoopDiagram","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractCausalLoopDiagram{S, Ts, P}\n\nAbstract supertype of ACSets whose schema contains SchCausalLoop. Every accessor in this package accepts one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.AbstractCausalLoopDiagram"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagramUntyped","page":"API Reference","text":"CausalLoopDiagramUntyped{Label, Sign, Flag}\n\nACSet type for SchCausalLoop with attribute types left open. Use CausalLoopDiagram for the standard instantiation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagramUntyped"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagram","page":"API Reference","text":"CausalLoopDiagram\n\nA causal loop diagram with Symbol factor names, Polarity link labels and Bool delay flags: CausalLoopDiagramUntyped{Symbol, Polarity, Bool}.\n\nBuild one with causal_loop_diagram from a list of links, or read one off a Bayesian network with causal_loop_diagram.\n\nExample\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Infection, :Defoliation) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Defoliation, :Vigour) => NEGATIVE,\n                                  delayed((:Vigour, :Infection) => NEGATIVE)]);\n\njulia> factor_names(cld)\n3-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :Infection\n :Defoliation\n :Vigour\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagram"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.LinkSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"LinkSpec(source, target, polarity; delay = false)\n\nOne link of a diagram as a value, which is what causal_loop_diagram takes a list of. (source, target) => polarity is read as one of these, and delayed marks it as crossing a delay.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.LinkSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.link","page":"API Reference","text":"link(source, target, polarity; delay = false) -> LinkSpec\n\nA link specification, the same thing as (source, target) => polarity with a name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.link"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.delayed","page":"API Reference","text":"delayed(spec) -> LinkSpec\n\nMark a link as crossing a delay: the effect of the source on the target is not felt in the same period. spec is a LinkSpec or a (source, target) => polarity pair.\n\nThe mark is what separates the two kinds of feedback loop. A loop with a delay in it is a loop a dynamic Bayesian network represents directly, the delayed link becoming a lagged input. A loop with no delay anywhere asserts that everything in it happens at once, and no Bayesian network, static or dynamic, can hold it without an extra assumption; see delayed_loops and instantaneous_loops.\n\njulia> delayed((:Vigour, :Infection) => NEGATIVE)\nLinkSpec(:Vigour, :Infection, -, true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.delayed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"API Reference","text":"causal_loop_diagram(links; factors = Symbol[]) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nThe diagram with these links, each a LinkSpec or a (source, target) => polarity pair. Factors appear in the order they are first mentioned, after any named in factors, which is how an isolated factor is included and how the order of the rows of polarity_matrix is fixed.\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Rain, :Grass) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Grass, :Sheep) => POSITIVE,\n                                  delayed((:Sheep, :Grass) => NEGATIVE)]);\n\njulia> nfactors(cld), nlinks(cld)\n(3, 3)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.add_factor!","page":"API Reference","text":"add_factor!(cld, name::Symbol) -> Int\n\nThe part id of the factor called name, adding it if the diagram does not have it yet. Idempotent, so a diagram never acquires two factors with the same name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.add_factor!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.add_link!","page":"API Reference","text":"add_link!(cld, source::Symbol, target::Symbol, polarity::Polarity; delay = false) -> Int\nadd_link!(cld, spec::LinkSpec) -> Int\n\nAdd a link and return its part id, adding either endpoint if the diagram does not carry it yet. A link parallel to an existing one is added as a second link rather than merged; use coarsen to combine them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.add_link!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.nfactors","page":"API Reference","text":"nfactors(cld) -> Int\n\nThe number of factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.nfactors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.nlinks","page":"API Reference","text":"nlinks(cld) -> Int\n\nThe number of links, counting parallel links separately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.nlinks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_names","page":"API Reference","text":"factor_names(cld) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe names of the factors in part-id order, which is the order they were added in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_name","page":"API Reference","text":"factor_name(cld, f::Integer) -> Symbol\n\nThe name of factor f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_name"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.has_factor","page":"API Reference","text":"has_factor(cld, name::Symbol) -> Bool\n\nWhether the diagram carries a factor of this name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.has_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_id","page":"API Reference","text":"factor_id(cld, name::Symbol) -> Int\n\nThe part id of the factor called name. Throws UnknownFactorError if there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.factor_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.link_table","page":"API Reference","text":"link_table(cld) -> Vector{<:NamedTuple}\n\nEvery link in part-id order as a named tuple (; source, target, polarity, delay), with its endpoints named. This is the form the figures and the vignette tables read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.link_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.has_link","page":"API Reference","text":"has_link(cld, source::Symbol, target::Symbol) -> Bool\n\nWhether the diagram has at least one link from source to target. Throws UnknownFactorError if either name is not a factor.\n\nThis is not the same question as whether link_polarity is NO_EFFECT: a link can be present and carry no effect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.has_link"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.link_polarity","page":"API Reference","text":"link_polarity(cld, source::Symbol, target::Symbol) -> Polarity\n\nThe polarity of the dependence of target on source: the sum, in the rig of Polarity, of the polarities of the parallel links between them. With no link at all the sum is empty and the answer is NO_EFFECT, the additive unit; use has_link to tell that case from a link that carries no effect.\n\nTwo parallel links of opposite sign give INDETERMINATE, which is the honest answer: the diagram says the two pathways disagree and says nothing about which wins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.link_polarity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.link_delay","page":"API Reference","text":"link_delay(cld, source::Symbol, target::Symbol) -> Bool\n\nWhether any link from source to target crosses a delay (see delayed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.link_delay"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_matrix","page":"API Reference","text":"polarity_matrix(cld) -> Matrix{Polarity}\n\nThe diagram as a square matrix over the rig of Polarity: entry (i, j) is the polarity of the link from factor i to factor j, in the order of factor_names, and NO_EFFECT where there is no link.\n\nMatrix multiplication over this rig then does what it does for an ordinary adjacency matrix: the (i, j) entry of the n-th power is the sum of the polarities of the paths of length n from i to j, so a diagonal entry of a power is a loop polarity, summed over the loops of that length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.delay_matrix","page":"API Reference","text":"delay_matrix(cld) -> Matrix{Bool}\n\nEntry (i, j) is whether any link from factor i to factor j crosses a delay, in the order of factor_names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.delay_matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.Loop","page":"API Reference","text":"Loop(factors, polarity, delay)\n\nAn elementary feedback loop: the factors in the order the loop visits them, starting from the one with the smallest part id, its polarity and whether it crosses a delay. The closing link runs from the last factor back to the first.\n\nA loop whose polarity is POSITIVE is reinforcing: a push on any factor in it comes back as a push the same way. A NEGATIVE loop is balancing: the push comes back the other way. A loop whose polarity is INDETERMINATE is a loop whose direction the diagram does not determine, and one whose polarity is NO_EFFECT runs through a link along which nothing moves, so it is not live.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.Loop"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.loops","page":"API Reference","text":"loops(cld; max_loops = 1_000) -> Vector{Loop}\n\nEvery elementary feedback loop of the diagram: every directed cycle that visits no factor twice, shortest first. Parallel links are summed first (see link_polarity), so each cycle of factors appears once, and a self-link is a loop of length one.\n\nEnumerating elementary cycles is exponential in the worst case; max_loops caps it and TooManyLoopsError says so.\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Hare, :Lynx) => POSITIVE,\n                                  delayed((:Lynx, :Hare) => NEGATIVE)]);\n\njulia> only(loops(cld))\nHare -> Lynx -> Hare (-, balancing, delayed)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.loops"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_polarity","page":"API Reference","text":"loop_polarity(cld, factors::AbstractVector{Symbol}) -> Polarity\n\nThe polarity of the loop that visits these factors in this order and closes from the last back to the first: the product of the polarities of its links. Throws NotALoopError if a factor repeats or a consecutive pair has no link, and UnknownFactorError if a name is not a factor of the diagram.\n\nBecause the rig is commutative the answer does not depend on which factor the sequence starts from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_polarity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_length","page":"API Reference","text":"loop_length(loop::Loop) -> Int\n\nThe number of links in the loop, which is also the number of factors it visits. A self-link is a loop of length one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_summary","page":"API Reference","text":"loop_summary(cld; max_loops = 1_000) -> NamedTuple\n\nCounts of the loops of a diagram: (; loops, reinforcing, balancing, undetermined, inactive, delayed, instantaneous). undetermined counts the loops whose polarity is INDETERMINATE and inactive those whose polarity is NO_EFFECT, so loops == reinforcing + balancing + undetermined + inactive and loops == delayed + instantaneous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.loop_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity-Tuple{Loop}","page":"API Reference","text":"polarity(loop::Loop) -> Polarity\n\nThe polarity of a loop: the product of the polarities of its links.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.polarity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.is_delayed","page":"API Reference","text":"is_delayed(loop::Loop) -> Bool\n\nWhether any link of the loop crosses a delay (see delayed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.is_delayed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.reinforcing","page":"API Reference","text":"reinforcing(loop::Loop) -> Bool\n\nWhether the loop is reinforcing, that is whether its polarity is POSITIVE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.reinforcing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.balancing","page":"API Reference","text":"balancing(loop::Loop) -> Bool\n\nWhether the loop is balancing, that is whether its polarity is NEGATIVE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.balancing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.delayed_loops","page":"API Reference","text":"delayed_loops(cld; max_loops = 1_000) -> Vector{Loop}\n\nThe loops with a delay somewhere in them: the loops a dynamic Bayesian network can hold as they stand, the delayed link becoming a lagged input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.delayed_loops"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.instantaneous_loops","page":"API Reference","text":"instantaneous_loops(cld; max_loops = 1_000) -> Vector{Loop}\n\nThe loops with no delay anywhere in them. These are the loops that assert simultaneous mutual dependence, and no Bayesian network holds one: a directed cycle in a belief network is rejected (BayesianNetworks.CyclicBayesNetError), and unrolling does not help, because there is no slice boundary to put the closing link across. Representing one means changing the model: separating the factor into stages, pushing it to equilibrium, or admitting a delay that the sketch left out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.instantaneous_loops"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram-Tuple{BayesModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"causal_loop_diagram(m::BayesModel; mode = :structural, unordered = Symbol[],\n                    reversed = Symbol[], atol = DEFAULT_ATOL, kwargs...)\n\nThe causal loop diagram of a model with its kernels bound: one factor per variable, in variable-id order, and one link per arc, labelled with the direction in which the arc moves (see polarity).\n\nmode picks which question the labels answer.\n\n:structural (the default) reads each mechanism's own table, one link per input, with the other parents held fixed. This is the interventional reading: the sign is what do_intervention on the parent would move. A variable wired to a mechanism twice gets two parallel links, which link_polarity then sums.\n:observational reads conditional(m, child, parent) for each arc of the graph instead, so the sign is the direction in which the two move together, confounding and all. This enumerates the joint, so it is capped the way BayesianNetworks.conditional is capped; kwargs (for instance max_states, evidence) are forwarded to it and are otherwise unused.\n\nThe declared order of a variable's states is what \"rises\" means, so two keywords say where that order is not the quantity the diagram is about.\n\nreversed names the variables whose states run the other way – declared best to worst when the diagram is about badness, say. Each reversed endpoint flips the sign of every link into and out of it.\nunordered names the variables whose states carry no order at all: a platform choice, a delivery route, a species identity. Every link touching one is INDETERMINATE, which is the truth. A diagram over a decision with incomparable options has little to say, and saying so is the point.\n\nUnbound mechanisms raise BayesianNetworks.MissingKernelError from kernel, unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram-Tuple{DynamicBayesModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"causal_loop_diagram(dm::DynamicBayesModel; kwargs...) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nThe causal loop diagram of a dynamic model's transition template, with the time index quotiented away: one factor per template variable, a lagged input X[t-k] of Y becoming a delayed link from X to Y, and a same-slice input an ordinary one.\n\nThis is the direction that recovers the loops. A dynamic Bayesian network is a Bayesian network, so its transition template is acyclic and carries no loop; collapsing the slices puts the loops back, and the ones that come back are exactly the delayed ones. Persistence of a variable from one slice to the next becomes a self-link, which is the shortest feedback loop there is.\n\nEquivalent to collapse_lags(causal_loop_diagram(transition_network(dm); kwargs...)); kwargs are those of causal_loop_diagram.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.causal_loop_diagram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.coarsen","page":"API Reference","text":"coarsen(cld, f; combine = true) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nThe diagram pushed forward along a map of factors: f sends each factor name to the name it becomes, as a Dict (names it does not mention are kept) or as a function. Links follow their endpoints, keeping their polarity and their delay.\n\nWith combine = true (the default) links that become parallel are merged into one, their polarities added in the rig of Polarity – so two pathways that disagree become one INDETERMINATE link – and the merged link is delayed if any of them was. With combine = false they are left side by side, which keeps every pathway visible; link_polarity adds them on reading either way.\n\nMerging two factors that were linked to each other turns that link into a self-link, which is a feedback loop of length one. That is not a defect of the operation: it is what coarsening a diagram does to the detail inside a factor.\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Spores, :Infection) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Conidia, :Infection) => POSITIVE]);\n\njulia> lumped = coarsen(cld, Dict(:Spores => :Inoculum, :Conidia => :Inoculum));\n\njulia> nfactors(lumped), nlinks(lumped)\n(2, 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.coarsen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.collapse_lags","page":"API Reference","text":"collapse_lags(cld) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nThe diagram with its time index quotiented away: a factor named X[t-k] becomes X, and a link that crosses a slice boundary becomes a delayed link. Names follow BayesianNetworks' lag convention, which lag_of parses.\n\nThis is the operation that turns a transition template back into a diagram with loops in it. The template is acyclic, as every Bayesian network must be; the loops are carried by the lags, and collapsing the index is what makes them visible again. A variable that persists from one slice to the next becomes a self-link.\n\nParallel links produced by the collapse are merged as in coarsen, so a variable that enters a mechanism both at the current slice and at a lag yields one link, delayed, whose polarity is the sum of the two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.collapse_lags"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.compare_diagrams","page":"API Reference","text":"compare_diagrams(a, b) -> DiagramComparison\n\nCompare two diagrams link by link: which links both have and agree on, which both have and disagree on, and which only one has. Parallel links are summed first, so the comparison is between the polarities link_polarity reports.\n\nThree uses, all of them checks that something has not been assumed.\n\nA hand-drawn sketch against the diagram read off the finished network says which links the modelling kept, which it silently re-signed, and which feedback it had to cut.\nThe :structural against the :observational reading of one network (see causal_loop_diagram) says where the direction two variables move together is not the direction intervening on one moves the other. Those links are the confounded ones, and a diagram drawn from observed co-movement has them wrong while claiming to be causal.\nA diagram against the collapse of a dynamic model's template (collapse_lags) says whether the loops the sketch drew are the loops the dynamic model closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.compare_diagrams"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.DiagramComparison","page":"API Reference","text":"DiagramComparison\n\nThe result of compare_diagrams: four lists of links, each entry naming its endpoints.\n\nagree: links both diagrams have, with the same polarity. Entries are (; source, target, polarity).\ndisagree: links both diagrams have with different polarities. Entries are (; source, target, first, second), the two polarities in the order the diagrams were given.\nonly_first, only_second: links only one diagram has, as (; source, target, polarity).\n\nonly_first is the interesting list when the first diagram is a sketch and the second was read off a network: it is what the network leaves out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.DiagramComparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.occurrences","page":"API Reference","text":"occurrences(motif, cld; induced = false, match_delay = false,\n            max_occurrences = 10_000) -> Vector{MotifOccurrence}\n\nEvery occurrence of motif in the diagram cld: every injective map from the motif's factors to the diagram's under which each link of the motif becomes a link of the diagram whose polarity the motif's label allows.\n\nBoth arguments are ordinary diagrams; a motif is a small labelled graph and nothing more, so causal_loop_diagram builds one, and motif names the standard shapes.\n\nA label matches by containment rather than equality (issubset on Polarity): a motif link labelled INDETERMINATE asks only that some link be there, whatever its sign, while one labelled POSITIVE matches a positive link alone. A host link that is itself INDETERMINATE matches only a motif link that is, because the diagram does not claim a sign for it.\n\ninduced = false (the default) lets the diagram carry further links among the matched factors: the question is whether the shape is present. With induced = true the matched factors must carry exactly the motif's links and no others.\n\nmatch_delay = false (the default) ignores delayed flags, because a motif is a question about shape; with match_delay = true each link must agree about its delay as well.\n\nThe map is injective, so distinct motif factors match distinct diagram factors: a chain of three does not occur in a loop of two. A motif with a symmetry occurs once per symmetry – :mutual_amplification has two occurrences in a single reinforcing pair, one for each way round – which is a property of counting maps, not a defect.\n\nThrows TooManyOccurrencesError past max_occurrences.\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Hare, :Lynx) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Lynx, :Hare) => NEGATIVE]);\n\njulia> only(occurrences(motif(:predator_prey), cld))\nA = Hare, B = Lynx\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.occurrences"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.count_occurrences","page":"API Reference","text":"count_occurrences(motif, cld; kwargs...) -> Int\n\nHow many times motif occurs in cld. Keywords are occurrences's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.count_occurrences"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.has_motif","page":"API Reference","text":"has_motif(motif, cld; induced = false, match_delay = false) -> Bool\n\nWhether motif occurs in cld at all. Stops at the first occurrence, so it is cheap and needs no cap.\n\njulia> cld = causal_loop_diagram([(:Load, :Defence) => POSITIVE,\n                                  (:Defence, :Load) => NEGATIVE]);\n\njulia> has_motif(motif(:predator_prey), cld), has_motif(motif(:self_balancing), cld)\n(true, false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.has_motif"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.MotifOccurrence","page":"API Reference","text":"MotifOccurrence\n\nOne occurrence of a motif in a diagram: the injective map from the motif's factors to the host's, as a list of (; motif, host) pairs in the motif's factor order.\n\nIndex it with a motif factor name to get the host factor it matched (occ[:A]), or take them all with host_factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.MotifOccurrence"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.host_factors","page":"API Reference","text":"host_factors(occ::MotifOccurrence) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe host factors the occurrence matched, in the motif's factor order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.host_factors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_factors","page":"API Reference","text":"motif_factors(occ::MotifOccurrence) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe motif's own factor names, in its factor order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_factors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.issubset-Tuple{Polarity, Polarity}","page":"API Reference","text":"issubset(a::Polarity, b::Polarity) -> Bool\n\nWhether every sign a admits is a sign b admits, in the subset order the four polarities carry by construction: {0}, {+} and {-} are the three atoms, each contained in itself and in {0, +, -} (INDETERMINATE) and in nothing else.\n\nIt is a partial order, not a total one – POSITIVE and NEGATIVE are incomparable – with INDETERMINATE as its greatest element. There is no least element, because the empty set is not one of the four.\n\nThis is the order a motif matches along (see occurrences), and the direction matters: a motif link matches a diagram's link when issubset(diagram_label, motif_label), the label found being one of the signs the motif will admit. So a motif link labelled INDETERMINATE asks only that some link be there and matches whatever sign it carries, while one labelled POSITIVE matches a positive link alone. The wildcard of the motif search is therefore not an extra value added for it, but the top of the rig that was already there.\n\nRead the other way the containment says something different and also true: a link which is genuinely INDETERMINATE does not match a motif asking for POSITIVE, because the diagram does not claim that sign.\n\njulia> issubset(POSITIVE, INDETERMINATE)\ntrue\n\njulia> issubset(INDETERMINATE, POSITIVE)\nfalse\n\njulia> issubset(POSITIVE, NEGATIVE)\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.issubset"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.MOTIF_LIBRARY","page":"API Reference","text":"MOTIF_LIBRARY\n\nThe standard shapes, as an OrderedDict from name to diagram. motif takes a copy of one by name and motif_names lists them.\n\nname shape what it says\n:self_reinforcing A -(+)-> A a factor that feeds itself: growth, or decline\n:self_balancing A -(-)-> A a factor that damps itself\n:mutual_amplification A -(+)-> B -(+)-> A each raises the other; reinforcing\n:mutual_suppression A -(-)-> B -(-)-> A each suppresses the other; reinforcing too\n:predator_prey A -(+)-> B -(-)-> A the balancing pair\n:chain A -(?)-> B -(?)-> C any two links in series\n:common_cause A -(?)-> B, A -(?)-> C one factor driving two: the confounding shape\n:common_effect A -(?)-> C, B -(?)-> C two factors meeting at one\n:opposed_pathways two routes A to D, signs + + and + - the shape whose coarsening is INDETERMINATE\n:agreeing_pathways two routes A to D, both + + the same shape that coarsens to a sign\n\nThe three with ? labels are shape-only and match any sign (see occurrences); they are the ones to count rather than to read a direction off.\n\n:mutual_suppression is in the library because it is the one practitioners misread: two negative links in series multiply to a positive loop, so mutual suppression is reinforcing, not balancing. :opposed_pathways and :agreeing_pathways are the same unlabelled shape with different signs, and they are worth telling apart because coarsen merges the first into an INDETERMINATE link and the second into a signed one: a sketch that lumps them loses a trade-off it cannot then state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.MOTIF_LIBRARY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.motif","page":"API Reference","text":"motif(name::Symbol) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nA copy of the named shape from MOTIF_LIBRARY. A copy, so that adding to what comes back cannot change the library.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError for a name the library does not hold.\n\njulia> nfactors(motif(:predator_prey)), nlinks(motif(:predator_prey))\n(2, 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.motif"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_names","page":"API Reference","text":"motif_names() -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe names in MOTIF_LIBRARY, in its order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_key","page":"API Reference","text":"motif_key(cld; match_delay = false) -> String\n\nA string that is the same for two diagrams exactly when they are isomorphic as labelled graphs: the canonical form, found by minimising over every relabelling of the factors. The factor names themselves are discarded, which is the point – a motif is a shape.\n\nThe key reads as the links of the canonical labelling, whose factors are A, B, C, ... in canonical order, so \"3: A+>B B->C\" is a three-factor chain, positive then negative.\n\nWith match_delay = true delays are part of the shape, so the same signed shape with and without a delay gets different keys; a delayed link is marked ~.\n\nBrute force over all relabellings, so it is for motifs: it throws an ArgumentError past seven factors.\n\njulia> a = causal_loop_diagram([(:Rain, :Runoff) => POSITIVE]);\n\njulia> b = causal_loop_diagram([(:Spores, :Infection) => POSITIVE]);\n\njulia> motif_key(a) == motif_key(b)\ntrue\n\njulia> motif_key(a)\n\"2: A+>B\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.canonical_motif","page":"API Reference","text":"canonical_motif(cld; match_delay = false) -> CausalLoopDiagram\n\nThe diagram relabelled into its canonical form: the same shape with its factors renamed A, B, C, ... so that two isomorphic diagrams come out identical. This is the representative motif_census reports, and motif_key is its string form.\n\nWith match_delay = false (the default) the delays are dropped, since they are not then part of the shape being counted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.canonical_motif"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_census","page":"API Reference","text":"motif_census(diagrams; size, match_delay = false, connected = true)\n    -> Vector{MotifCount}\n\nThe shapes of size factors that occur across a collection of diagrams, counted by how many diagrams contain each: MotifCount rows, the widest support first.\n\ndiagrams is a Dict of name to diagram, a vector of name => diagram pairs, or a plain vector (named d1, d2, ... in order).\n\nThe census is induced, which is the opposite of occurrences's default and is deliberate: each set of size factors contributes exactly one shape, the one it actually carries, so the rows partition the sets counted and the occurrences column adds up. Were it not induced, a set carrying a loop would also be counted as each chain inside it, and a shape's count would say more about the vocabulary of shapes than about the diagrams.\n\nconnected = true (the default) keeps only those sets whose factors all touch a link inside the set and which hang together as an undirected graph. For size = 1 that leaves exactly the self-links, which is the useful one-factor question.\n\nWith match_delay = true a delay is part of the shape, so the same signed shape counts separately according to whether it crosses one.\n\nWhat the result is for, and what it is not. support is the interesting column, and a shape supported by every diagram is usually telling you about the vocabulary rather than the subject – a bare link occurs everywhere. The counts are of factor sets, not of causal claims, and a polarity carries no magnitude, so a recurring shape is a question worth asking of each diagram rather than a finding on its own.\n\njulia> a = causal_loop_diagram([(:X, :Y) => POSITIVE, (:Y, :X) => NEGATIVE]);\n\njulia> b = causal_loop_diagram([(:P, :Q) => POSITIVE, (:Q, :P) => NEGATIVE]);\n\njulia> only(motif_census([:a => a, :b => b]; size=2))\n2: A+>B B->A  in 2 diagrams, 2 times\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.motif_census"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.MotifCount","page":"API Reference","text":"MotifCount\n\nOne row of a motif_census: a shape, and where it was found.\n\nkey: the canonical form as a string (motif_key).\nmotif: the shape itself, canonically labelled (canonical_motif).\nsupport: how many diagrams of the collection contain it. This is the column to read: a shape in one diagram is that diagram's business, and a shape in most of them is a property of the subject rather than of any one model.\noccurrences: how many times in total, summed over the collection.\ndiagrams: the names of the diagrams that contain it, in the order given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.MotifCount"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BayesianNetworks.to_graphviz-Tuple{AbstractCausalLoopDiagram}","page":"API Reference","text":"to_graphviz(cld::AbstractCausalLoopDiagram; kwargs...) -> Graphviz.Graph\n\nDraw a causal loop diagram: one node per factor and one edge per link, the edge labelled with the polarity's symbol (+, -, 0 or ?), coloured green for POSITIVE, red for NEGATIVE and grey for the other two, and dashed when the link crosses a delay.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nhighlight: a Loop, or a vector of factor names read as one, whose links are drawn heavier. This is how a figure points at the loop the text is about.\nrankdir = \"LR\", name = \"G\", and graph_attrs, node_attrs, edge_attrs (dictionaries merged over the defaults), as in BayesianNetworks' own to_graphviz.\n\nRender with show(io, MIME\"image/svg+xml\"(), g) or BayesianNetworks.Graphviz.run_graphviz, which is how the vignettes write their PNGs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesianNetworks.to_graphviz"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagramsError","page":"API Reference","text":"CausalLoopDiagramsError\n\nAbstract supertype of every exception that CausalLoopDiagrams defines: UnknownFactorError, NotALoopError, TooManyLoopsError and TooManyOccurrencesError. It marks the package that introduced an error, not a kind of failure. Invalid arguments and keywords raise Base's ArgumentError instead, which is outside this root, and the typed errors of BayesianNetworks and FiniteKernels pass through unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.CausalLoopDiagramsError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.UnknownFactorError","page":"API Reference","text":"UnknownFactorError(name, known)\n\nThrown when a factor name is looked up that the diagram does not carry. known is the diagram's factor names, so the message can show what was available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.UnknownFactorError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.NotALoopError","page":"API Reference","text":"NotALoopError(factors, msg)\n\nThrown when a sequence of factor names is read as a loop but is not one: a repeated factor, or a consecutive pair with no link between them. factors is the sequence as given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.NotALoopError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.TooManyLoopsError","page":"API Reference","text":"TooManyLoopsError(found, max_loops)\n\nThrown by loops when a diagram has more elementary loops than max_loops. Enumerating loops is exponential in the worst case, so the cap is a guard rather than a limit of the algorithm; raise it deliberately.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.TooManyLoopsError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CausalLoopDiagrams.TooManyOccurrencesError","page":"API Reference","text":"TooManyOccurrencesError(found, max_occurrences)\n\nThrown by occurrences when a motif occurs in a diagram more often than max_occurrences. Subgraph matching is exponential in the size of the motif, so the cap is a guard rather than a limit of the algorithm; raise it deliberately.\n\nA motif that occurs this often is usually a motif that says nothing – a bare link, or a chain of INDETERMINATE links, which matches everywhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.TooManyOccurrencesError"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Works cited in the docstrings and in the tutorials. The bibliography file is docs/src/references.bib, synced from the workspace-level docs/references.bib by scripts/sync_references.jl.\n\nBaez, J. C. (2024). Polarities. Azimuth (blog), parts 1 to 6. The series this package follows: polarities as elements of a monoid,                   labellings as functors, and the move to rings, hyperfields and rigs.\n\n\n\nBaez, J. C. and Chaudhuri, A. (2025). Graphs With Polarities, arXiv:2506.23375 [math.CT]. Submitted 29 June 2025; revised March 2026.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#CausalLoopDiagrams.jl","page":"Home","text":"Causal loop diagrams as labelled graphs, and the bridge between them and the Bayesian networks of the ecorecipes ecosystem (CausalLoopDiagrams → BayesianNetworks → FiniteKernels).\n\nA causal loop diagram is the first thing a group of people draw when they describe a system: factors joined by arrows, each arrow marked with the direction in which it pushes, and loops closing back on themselves. Baez formalises one as a graph whose edges are labelled by elements of a monoid of polarities, which makes a labelling the same thing as a functor from the free category on the graph into the delooping of the monoid (Baez, 2024); Baez and Chaudhuri extend this to three kinds of morphism between labelled graphs – refining, simplifying, finding motifs – and to a homology that counts the feedback a composite acquires that neither part had (Baez and Chaudhuri, 2025).\n\nA Bayesian network carries much more than a sign per arrow, and one thing less: it must be acyclic, so it cannot hold a loop at all. This package is the translation in both directions.","title":"CausalLoopDiagrams.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-polarities","page":"Home","text":"Polarity has four values, not the three of Baez's {+, 0, -}:\n\nvalue prints meaning\nPOSITIVE + the child moves the same way as the parent\nNEGATIVE - the child moves the opposite way\nNO_EFFECT 0 the child does not depend on the parent\nINDETERMINATE ? it depends which way, so the diagram does not say\n\nThe fourth and the third are distinct here because a conditional probability table tells them apart, where a whiteboard sketch cannot. The four are the subsets {0}, {+}, {-} and {0, +, -} of the sign hyperfield, closed under its multiplication and its multivalued addition, so they form a commutative rig: * composes links along a path and + combines links in parallel. The rig, rather than just a monoid, is what coarsen needs, because merging two factors must combine the labels of the links that become parallel.","title":"The polarities"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reading-a-sign-off-a-table","page":"Home","text":"polarity tests stochastic monotonicity in the declared order of the states: the child's distribution rises with the parent if its upper tail never shrinks, for every configuration of the other parents. Monotone kernels compose, which is what makes this a labelling in Baez's sense rather than a convention – the polarity of a path really is the product of the polarities of its links, and test/test_polarity.jl checks that against compose_kernel directly.\n\nTwo keywords say where the declared order is not the quantity the diagram is about: reversed for a variable whose states run best to worst, and unordered for one whose states carry no order at all, every link touching which comes out INDETERMINATE.","title":"Reading a sign off a table"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-two-kinds-of-loop","page":"Home","text":"using CausalLoopDiagrams\n\nsketch = causal_loop_diagram([(:Inoculum, :Infection) => POSITIVE,\n                              (:Infection, :Defoliation) => POSITIVE,\n                              (:Defoliation, :Vigour) => NEGATIVE,\n                              delayed((:Vigour, :Infection) => NEGATIVE),\n                              delayed((:Infection, :Inoculum) => POSITIVE)])\nloops(sketch)\n\ndelayed is the mark that matters for modelling, and it splits the feedback in two.\n\nA loop with a delay in it (delayed_loops) is one a dynamic Bayesian network holds as it stands: the delayed link becomes a lagged input, and causal_loop_diagram recovers the loop from a transition template by collapsing the time index (collapse_lags). Persistence of a variable from one slice to the next comes back as a self-link, the shortest feedback loop there is.\nA loop with no delay anywhere (instantaneous_loops) asserts that everything in it happens at once. No Bayesian network holds one, and unrolling does not help, because there is no slice boundary to put the closing link across. Representing one means changing the model.","title":"The two kinds of loop"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-the-translation-discards","page":"Home","text":"Worth stating plainly, because it is why a diagram is a sketch and a network a model. A polarity carries no magnitude, so it cannot say which of two loops through one factor wins – which is why Baez's series moves on to rings, hyperfields and rigs with more in them than signs. It carries no interaction: a link whose sign depends on another parent comes out INDETERMINATE, and a lever worth more alongside an intervention than alone has no expression in a diagram at all. And it carries no unordered variable, which is what most choices between incomparable options are.","title":"What the translation discards"},{"category":"section","location":"#Two-readings-of-one-network","page":"Home","text":"causal_loop_diagram reads a model either way, and compare_diagrams says where they differ:\n\n:structural reads each mechanism's own table, so the sign is what intervening on the parent would move;\n:observational reads conditional(m, child, parent), so the sign is the direction in which the two move together in this model.\n\nThey part company exactly where the network is confounded. A causal loop diagram drawn from observed co-movement carries the second while claiming the first, so the disagreement list is a soundness check on a diagram someone else drew.","title":"Two readings of one network"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\", \"references.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Contents"}]
}
